Sunsets are the west coast's daily ritual. The coast looks out over the open Indian Ocean and the air is usually clearer and drier than in the east. On a good evening the sky turns gold then pink then deep blue over the water. These are the places we like best.

## Tamarin Bay

At [Tamarin Bay](/en/places/tamarin-bay) the sun sinks over the open sea beyond the surf break while the mountains behind the village catch the last light. Find a spot on the public beach and watch the surfers come in. Stay for the colours after the sun has gone.

## La Preneuse

Smaller and quieter [La Preneuse beach](/en/places/la-preneuse-beach) at Black River is where many locals come at the end of the day. The silhouette of the old Martello tower against the evening sky makes it especially photogenic.

## Flic en Flac

The long beach at [Flic en Flac](/en/places/flic-en-flac-public-beach) faces due west so wherever you sit the sun sets in front of you. It is the liveliest option with families on the sand and food stalls nearby.

## Le Morne

On the west side of the peninsula [Le Morne public beach](/en/places/le-morne-public-beach) adds the outline of the mountain to the scene. In the evening light the lagoon turns from turquoise to silver.

## Timing and season

- Mauritius is in the tropics so twilight is short. The sun sets between about a quarter to six and seven in the evening all year round. It sets earlier in the southern winter (June and July) and later in summer (December and January).
- Arrive half an hour before sunset and stay twenty minutes after: the strongest colours often come once the sun has dropped below the horizon.
- The dry southern winter tends to bring clearer skies. In summer clouds can make for dramatic (or hidden) sunsets.
- Bring a light layer and insect repellent for after dark.
